      "text": "Let's first understand what makes a lucky integer:\n1. Definition: A lucky integer is any positive integer number that is divisible by the sum of its individual digits.\n\n1. Calculation: We will write a procedure according to the definition in order to find the least positive multiple of 9 that is not a lucky integer.\n\n1. Procedure: Begin with a number, say 'number', equals to 9.\n1. Check: To see if the number is a lucky integer, compute the sum of its digits and check if 'number' is divisible by the digit sum. If 'number' is not divisible by the sum of its digits, it's not a lucky integer.\n1. Increment: If 'number' is not a lucky integer, increase 'number' by 9.\n1. The result: Repeat the procedure until finding the least multiple of 9 that is not a lucky integer.\n\nHere's the Python code to do this:\n```python\ndef digit_sum(n):\n    return sum(int(x) for x in str(n))\n\ndef is_lucky(n):\n    return n % digit_sum(n) == 0\n\ndef least_multiple_of_9_not_lucky():\n    number = 9\n    while True:\n        if not is_lucky(number):\n            return number\n        number += 9\n\nleast_multiple_of_9_not_lucky()\n```\nThe result is the least multiple of 9 that is not a lucky integer. Let's debug this Python program to find the least multiple of 9 that meets this condition.\n\n1. Check the code snippet line by line:\n    - function is_lucky calculates the sum of the digits of a number.\n    - The function least_multiple_of_9_not_lucky starts at 9 and iteratively adds 9 and checks if the current number is not lucky. It keeps going until it finds a number that meets the criterion.\n\n1. Execution: When we run the above code, it will iteratively increase 'number' by 9 until it detects the least multiple of 9 that is not a lucky integer. Run the Python script to find the answer.\n\nLet's test our solution in the Python code. The output should be the smallest positive multiple of 9 that is not a lucky integer. This involves checking incrementing multiples by 9 and verifying if it is a lucky integer. Begin with 'start' = 9 and keep incrementing till the criterion is checked and meeting the condition of being least among multiples of 9. Thus, our procedure returns the lowest 'start' value which doesn't satisfy the condition for \"lucky integer\". \n\nGiven Python code to find \"Least multiple of 9 NOT a Lucky Integer\":\n```python\ndef digit_sum(n):\n    return sum(int(x) for x in str(n))\n\ndef is_lucky(n):\n    return n % digit_sum(n) == 0\n\ndef least_multiple_of_9_not_lucky(start=9):\n    while True:\n        if not is_lucky(start):\n            return start\n        start += 9\n\nprint(least_multiple_of_9_not_lucky())\n```",
